WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.590
"There's a breathless hush in the
Close to-night—" Ten runs to make.

2
00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.220
The last man in.

3
00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.460
His captain's hand falls
on his shoulder: "Play up!

4
00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:10.060
play up!

5
00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.710
and play the game!"

6
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:12.780
Hard cut.

7
00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:14.070
A desert battle.

8
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.600
Dust and smoke.

9
00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:17.270
The square has broken.

10
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.310
And the same words ring out.

11
00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.860
A school cricket match, and a battle.

12
00:00:21.910 --> 00:00:24.700
Why does the poem give
them the same words?

13
00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.750
In this mission, you'll visit the Close,
the cricket ground of Clifton College,

14
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.980
where Henry Newbolt, who wrote it in 1892,

15
00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:33.350
went to school.

16
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.420
You'll follow the refrain
that ends every stanza.

17
00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:39.560
And you'll find out why
it's called Vitaï Lampada:

18
00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:41.300
the torch of life.

19
00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:45.500
Your mission: explain why
the poem uses the same words.
